{"id":"smith_sumo_squat","name":"Smith sumo squat","cue":"Toes out, knees over toes; sit straight down between your legs.","research":"Smith machine sumo squat (science audit table B: the wide-stance squat as the seated hip adduction alternative; a deep wide squat reaches the adductors and glutes more than a narrow one, Kubo 2019). Wide stance: soles flat on a floor plate 0.2 H either side of the midline (about 1.6x shoulder width, mid-sole), toes out 45 deg (foot IK), the knees pointed over the toes (hip abduction 35 deg at the bottom, inside the 45 deg cap). Bar on the upper back (high bar, the trapezius: skin 0.004 H under the bar at every phase, the same bar point in the thorax frame as the Smith squat), rails 0.09 H behind the mid-sole line, hands 0.26 H each side as in the Smith squat. Bottom: knee 120, hip flexion 107 (thighs below parallel), ankle 25, trunk 20 deg forward of vertical with the spine neutral (spinal flexion 6). Top: standing with soft knees (8), trunk 5. The hip height is solved from the knee angle at both ends and runs linearly between; the hip depth follows the bar (a bar point fixed on the trapezius, rails vertical). Muscles: inner thighs (adductors) and glutes primary, the quads amber.","targets":{"tol":10,"tolBy":{"elbow_flexion":15,"shoulder_elevation":15,"ankle_dorsiflexion":12},"note":"The hands hold the bar behind the neck with the elbows down; elbow and shoulder angles are the IK solution for a 0.26 H grip. The top ankle reads about -7 deg because the abducted, toed-out foot is solved flat on the plate.","A":{"trunk_flexion":20,"hip_flexion":107,"hip_abduction":35,"knee_flexion":120,"ankle_dorsiflexion":25},"B":{"trunk_flexion":5,"hip_flexion":7,"hip_abduction":17,"knee_flexion":8,"ankle_dorsiflexion":-7}},"muscles":{"primary":["adductors","glute_max"],"secondary":["vast_lat","rect_fem","vast_med"],"side":"both"},"camera":{"az":90,"el":10,"dAz":-40,"dEl":2,"fill":0.62,"note":"Research view az 90 (profile: depth and trunk lean). Turne